1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,390
We are trying to connect massive government data sets in your ministry, right?

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:08,690
Mm-hmm. But this environment relies on a surprisingly small set of single purpose components.

3
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:09,930
Why is that necessary?

4
00:00:10,790 --> 00:00:16,650
Well, what's fascinating here is that strictly limiting each component's function actually isolates the risk.

5
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:20,570
It establishes trust through three core components.

6
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:22,130
Okay, what is the first one?

7
00:00:22,970 --> 00:00:26,890
First is the central server. So this is operated by the federation owner.

8
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:28,870
Right, like the master registry.

9
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:31,650
Exactly. The registry of members and services.

10
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:34,890
Every single other server has to check with it.

11
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:40,070
Then second, you have the Test CA, or Test Certificate Authority.

12
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:42,710
And that acts as the trust anchor, right?

13
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:43,790
Issuing the certificates.

14
00:00:44,270 --> 00:00:46,770
Yeah, because trust requires a synchronized timeline.

15
00:00:47,710 --> 00:00:50,910
A tamper-proof clock basically stamping every interaction.

16
00:00:51,790 --> 00:00:53,990
Ah, so you can prove exactly when a request was made.

17
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:54,750
You got it.

18
00:00:55,570 --> 00:00:58,050
And finally, there are the security servers.

19
00:00:58,610 --> 00:01:02,690
These are the dedicated gateways that really carry the trust burden at each edge.

20
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:09,050
Meaning the federation owner gets one, and then members like the identity authority or the learner registry, they get their own.

21
00:01:09,410 --> 00:01:11,890
Yes, exactly. The examination authority too.

22
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:14,430
They each sit behind their own dedicated gateway.

23
00:01:15,490 --> 00:01:18,710
Wait, so you cannot just power everything on simultaneously, right?

24
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:22,350
It requires a pretty strict sequential recipe to actually establish that trust.

25
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:22,510
So

26
00:01:23,170 --> 00:01:24,910
what is that exact sequence?

27
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:26,330
It is very strict.

28
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:29,630
Standing it up is a measured runbook exercise.

29
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:32,430
You have to do it explicitly in this order.

30
00:01:32,710 --> 00:01:33,470
Okay, lay it out for me.

31
00:01:34,170 --> 00:01:36,170
So you start with the central server. Got it.

32
00:01:36,690 --> 00:01:37,890
Then the Test CA,

33
00:01:38,749 --> 00:01:39,910
then the members on the registry,

34
00:01:40,610 --> 00:01:41,150
then the anchor.

35
00:01:41,910 --> 00:01:43,170
Right, so the foundations are set.

36
00:01:43,570 --> 00:01:43,970
Exactly.

37
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:46,030
Then each security server,

38
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:49,930
and its certificate and registration have to be approved on the central server.

39
00:01:50,270 --> 00:01:53,170
Makes sense. And then finally, the services and the grants.

40
00:01:53,630 --> 00:01:58,350
So when you actually run this build pack from zero, what are the tangible results we see on screen?

41
00:01:59,230 --> 00:02:02,830
We can break the live deployment down into four distinct observations.

42
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:03,990
Okay, what is the first?

43
00:02:04,610 --> 00:02:07,710
First, you will see six containers are all healthy.

44
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:12,370
That is one central server, one Test CA, and four security servers.

45
00:02:12,910 --> 00:02:15,190
Wow, okay. And the second observation.

46
00:02:15,910 --> 00:02:18,890
Second, you see that the deployment is incredibly measured.

47
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:24,590
It takes about a hundred seconds until healthy, and six and a half minutes from zero to a running federation.

48
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:28,410
Six and a half minutes. Right. That is fast.

49
00:02:28,630 --> 00:02:28,790
Right.

50
00:02:29,410 --> 00:02:33,050
Third, you see three members securely behind their own security servers.

51
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:37,650
That is the learner registry, the examination authority, and the identity authority.

52
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:40,050
Nice. And the fourth observation.

53
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:48,270
Fourth, everything is exact. Clients are registered, grants explicitly allow only the right authorities, and monitoring is running.

54
00:02:48,970 --> 00:02:49,790
So what does this all mean?

55
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:55,230
It means the bus exists. It is ready to carry real calls between your ministry's departments.

56
00:02:55,610 --> 00:02:57,950
Yeah. Preventing your systems from becoming

57
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:01,970
locked into one supplier or, you know, costly to change.

58
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:02,890
Exactly.

